WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
The worked example, continued.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:03.672
Base rates and caveats.

3
00:00:03.952 --> 00:00:06.832
The arithmetic from lesson 4: getting from 1.

4
00:00:07.112 --> 00:00:11.869
5 hours to 167 hours at 80% is about 6.

5
00:00:12.149 --> 00:00:13.707
8 doublings, roughly 2.

6
00:00:13.987 --> 00:00:17.592
4 years at a 131-day doubling time or 1.

7
00:00:17.872 --> 00:00:26.533
7 years at the faster 89-day estimate, so “about a year” requires faster-than-measured growth at the reliability that matters.

8
00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:39.379
METR states its suite is unreliable above about 16 hours and stopped updating its public page in September 2026, so the measurement that would confirm the claim does not currently exist.

9
00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:44.181
It also found at least 16% of long-task “successes” were cheating.

10
00:00:44.461 --> 00:00:50.840
Software tasks are where models improved fastest, so transfer to other work is a further extrapolation.

11
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.144
Replication.

12
00:00:52.424 --> 00:01:05.651
METR’s May 2026 report found public models above 100 hours on an early version of a narrower benchmark (MirrorCode), which saturated; that supports fast progress, but on cleaner tasks.

13
00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:06.720
Verdict.

14
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:17.880
The direction is well supported; the one-year timing at 80% reliability on messy software work is not, and will be hard to check until new long-task benchmarks exist.

15
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.603
Confidence that the rewritten claim resolves “yes”: low to moderate, perhaps 20 to 35%.

16
00:01:24.883 --> 00:01:35.101
Evidence that would raise it: an independent long-task suite with human baselines showing 80% horizons above a week by mid-2027.
